<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Ajayi Crowther</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1809–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Ajayi Crowther was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> linguist and clergyman who was freed from a slave ship as a boy and educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. He worked as a missionary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a>, translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> into Yoruba and led the Church Missionary Society's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> mission, founding a station at Onitsha in 1857. In 1864 he became the first African Anglican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-ajayi-crowther.jpg" alt="Samuel Ajayi Crowther" width="320" height="565" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bishop_Samuel_Ajayi_Crowther_1867.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ajayi was born in Osogun, in present-day Oyo State, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, and was a grandson of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abiodun">Abiodun</a> through his mother, Afala. In March 1821, when he was about twelve, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> slave raiders captured him with his family and his whole village during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> civil wars, and he was sold to Portuguese slave traders. His father, Ayemi, was most likely killed in the raid. Before the ship left port it was boarded by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> crew under Captain Henry Leeke, and the captives were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> the Church Missionary Society taught him to read and write English. He was baptised on 11 December 1825, taking the name of Samuel Crowther, vicar of Christ Church, Newgate. After a year at the school of St Mary's Church in Islington he returned in 1827 and became the first student of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourah-bay-college">Fourah Bay College</a>, where he studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Greek and Temne and later taught.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1841 Crowther accompanied the missionary James Schön on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> expedition, which aimed to stimulate commerce, encourage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and help end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. Recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, he was ordained by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-townsend">Henry Townsend</a> he opened a mission at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abeokuta">Abeokuta</a>, in today's Ogun State. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> grammar was published in 1843, and A Vocabulary of the Yoruba Language, with many local proverbs, appeared in London in 1852. After the expeditions of 1854 and 1857 he published an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> primer in 1857, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> primer in 1860 and a full Nupe grammar and vocabulary in 1864.</p><p>He was consecrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> by Charles Longley, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, at Canterbury Cathedral on St Peter's day in 1864, and later received the degree of Doctor of Divinity from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> he and Captain James Pinson Labulo Davies founded The Academy on 24 October 1866.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Crowther continued to supervise the translation of the Yoruba Bible, completed in the mid-1880s. He married Asano, baptised Susan, who had also been freed from a Portuguese slave ship; their son Dandeson Coates Crowther became archdeacon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a>, and their grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-macaulay">Herbert Macaulay</a> was one of the first Nigerian nationalists. Crowther died of a stroke in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> on 31 December 1891 and was buried at Ajele Cemetery. His remains were reburied in 1976, and a cenotaph stands at the Cathedral Church of Christ, Lagos. Some Anglican churches, including the Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, keep his feast on 31 December.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel Ajayi Crowther” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In West Africa, meanwhile, Christianity and Islam spread rapidly among the Yoruba from the 19th century: missionaries arrived in Abeokuta in the 1840s, and the Yoruba <b>Samuel Ajayi Crowther</b>, a freed slave, became the first African Anglican bishop in 1864, while Islam spread from the north.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Returned freed slaves from Sierra Leone, known as Saro, and missionaries such as Henry Townsend and <b>Samuel Ajayi Crowther</b> made Abeokuta a centre of Christianity and Western education.</blockquote><a href="/state/egba-united-government">Egba United Government</a></li><li><blockquote>Anglican missionaries under <b>Samuel Ajayi Crowther</b> arrived in 1857.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-onitsha">Kingdom of Onitsha</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Iseyin, in what is now <a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Died in Lagos, in what is now <a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=7.97,3.6&amp;year=1862" aria-label="Iseyin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4650 1866 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Samuel Ajayi Crowther"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5100 2136h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5100 2136h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=7.97,3.6&amp;year=1862">What was here in 1862? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
